## Why This Is Asked
This question probes handling of real-time financial flows, idempotency, and resilience to transient backend failures in end-to-end tests, including deterministic timing and data isolation.

## Key Concepts
- E2E testing with simulated external services (broker API, WebSocket feed)
- Idempotency keys and retry/backoff strategies
- Real-time UI updates via WebSocket streams
- Test data isolation per user and deterministic timing

## Code Example
```javascript
// Playwright pseudo-test sketch
test('real-time order flow with idempotency', async ({page}) => {
  // seed user data
  await seedUser(page, 'user1');
  // place order with idempotency key
  const idKey = 'abc-123';
  await page.route('**/broker/orders', route => {
    // first call 429, then 200
  });
  await page.click('#place-market-order');
  // verify PENDING, then WebSocket update to FILLED
});
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test multiple concurrent order submissions from a single user?
- How would you simulate broker outages and measure UI degradation versus data integrity?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests around feature flags in critical flows like checkout are common in large apps. This question probes how to manage multiple UI branches, data isolation per tenant, and end-to-end integrity, including idempotency and analytics reconciliation.

## Key Concepts

- Feature flags and environment isolation
- Deterministic testing with mocks and fake timers
- Idempotent purchases and event reconciliation
- Analytics/instrumentation validation across retries

## Code Example

```javascript
// Playwright test skeleton for flag-gated checkout
import { test, expect } from '@playwright/test';

test.describe('Flag-gated checkout', () => {
  test('flag ON renders new UI and processes purchase', async ({ page }) => {
    // mock flag service to ON for tenant A
    await page.route('**/flags/**', route =>
      route.fulfill({ status: 200, body: JSON.stringify({ flag: 'on' }) })
    );
    // proceed with purchase flow, using idempotent key
    // assert UI contains new checkout and purchase succeeds
  });

  test('flag OFF renders legacy UI and processes same purchase', async ({ page }) => {
    // mock flag service to OFF for tenant A
    await page.route('**/flags/**', route =>
      route.fulfill({ status: 200, body: JSON.stringify({ flag: 'off' }) })
    );
    // proceed with same purchase flow, ensure success and analytics
  });
});
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you scale this to many tenants and multiple flag states in parallel?
- How would you verify that analytics events are eventually ingested and deduplicated?

## Why This Is Asked

